course: biology
lesson: 98
title: The butterfly house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The butterfly house is warm and damp like a rainforest, because butterflies are cold-blooded and the tropical kinds need warmth to fly.
explain: A blue morpho flashes bright blue, then vanishes into brown when it closes its wings; another butterfly stares at you with a big fake eye; a glasswing is almost invisible against the leaves.
explain: Butterflies cannot chew: they uncurl a long tube called a proboscis to drink from orange slices and rotting bananas, and they taste with their feet when they land.
explain: If one lands on your sleeve, let it be, because its wings are covered in tiny scales like dust that rub off easily.
explain: In The Very Hungry Caterpillar the caterpillar eats cake and sausage and builds a cocoon, but real caterpillars eat only their own food plants, and butterflies make a chrysalis.
fact: Butterfly houses are kept warm and humid; butterflies need a body temperature of about 30 degrees to fly well, so they bask in the sun.
fact: The blue morpho's wings are bright blue on top and brown with eye spots underneath; the blue comes from tiny structures on the scales that reflect blue light, not from pigment.
fact: Butterfly wings are covered in tiny scales that rub off easily, and losing many can make flying harder; Lepidoptera, the name for butterflies and moths, means «scaly wings».
fact: Butterflies drink nectar, fruit juice and water through a long proboscis that uncurls like a party blower, and they taste with their feet.
fact: Inside a chrysalis the caterpillar's body breaks down and is rebuilt as a butterfly; cocoons are spun by moths, not butterflies; butterfly houses often buy pupae from butterfly farms in tropical countries, where local people earn money while keeping the forest standing.
fact: Owl butterflies have large eye spots on the underside of their wings that may startle birds or draw their pecks away from the body.
fact: The atlas moth is one of the biggest moths, as big as a plate; adults have no mouth, do not eat and live only one or two weeks on fat stored as caterpillars.
fact: The glasswing butterfly has clear patches on its wings with few scales, which makes it hard to see against leaves and flowers.
fact: Monarch butterflies fly thousands of kilometres from Canada and the USA to mountains in Mexico for winter, and it takes several generations to fly north again.
fact: Monarchs taste bad to birds because their caterpillars eat poisonous milkweed.
fact: Many caterpillars eat only one or a few kinds of plant, so gardens with the right plants help; nettles help red admiral and peacock butterflies in Britain.
fact: Many tropical butterflies drink from rotting fruit and mud puddles to get sugar and salts, and in the Amazon some even drink turtles' tears.
fact: Butterflies are insects with six legs and four wings, but in many kinds, like monarchs and peacocks, the front pair of legs is tiny, so they seem to stand on four.
exercise: Why is the butterfly house kept so warm? | Butterflies need warmth to fly | The plants would die in the cold | Warmth keeps the hungry birds away | Butterflies like to sweat a lot
exercise: What body temperature do butterflies need to fly well? | About 30 degrees | Just about 0 degrees | About 100 degrees | Only about 10 degrees
exercise: Why does a blue morpho seem to vanish when it lands? | Its wings are brown underneath | It turns see-through | It flies far too fast to see | It hides under leaves
exercise: Where does the morpho's bright blue come from? | Tiny structures on the scales that reflect blue light | Blue paint-like pigment in the wings | The blue flowers it drinks nectar from every day | The blue sky reflecting off it
exercise: Why should you not touch a butterfly's wings? | Tiny scales rub off and flying gets harder | The wings are covered in poison that burns your fingers | The butterfly will bite you | The wings will turn brown
exercise: What does the name Lepidoptera mean? | Scaly wings | Six small legs | Pretty flier | Tasting feet
exercise: What does a butterfly drink with? | A long tube called a proboscis | Its sharp, strong jaws for biting | Its front legs | Its feelers
exercise: What can butterflies taste with? | Their feet | Their wings | Their big eyes | Their tails
exercise: What happens inside a chrysalis? | The caterpillar's body breaks down and is rebuilt | The caterpillar just sleeps, grows wings and wakes up | A butterfly lays its eggs | The caterpillar eats leaves stored inside
exercise: How can a big eye spot on a wing help a butterfly? | It may startle birds or draw pecks away from the body | It is a real eye that lets the butterfly see behind it | It keeps the wing warm | It attracts flowers to open
exercise: Why does a grown-up atlas moth not eat? | It has no mouth and lives on fat stored as a caterpillar | It eats only at night, when nobody is there to see it | It is too big to fit on flowers | It drinks only rainwater
exercise: What is special about a glasswing butterfly's wings? | They have clear patches with few scales | They are made of real glass, so they snap easily | They glow in the dark | They are heavier than a bird's
exercise: Where do monarch butterflies fly for the winter? | Mountains in Mexico | The ice of the Arctic | The deserts of Africa | The middle of the ocean
exercise: Why do birds avoid eating monarchs? | Their caterpillars eat poisonous milkweed | Monarchs bite back hard | Monarchs are too fast to catch | Birds are afraid of anything coloured bright orange
exercise: Why does planting the right plants in a garden help butterflies? | Many caterpillars eat only one or a few kinds of plant | Butterflies sleep inside the flowers at night to keep warm | The plants keep birds away | Butterflies eat the roots
exercise: Why do some butterflies drink from rotting fruit and mud? | To get sugar and salts | To clean their dusty wings | To cool down their feet | To lay their eggs in the mud
test: 3 butterflies on the orange dish. how many wings, quick | 12 | 6 | 9 | 18
test: How many legs does a monarch butterfly really have? | Six, though the front two are tiny | Four, like a cat | Eight long legs, like a spider | Two, like a bird
test: In The Very Hungry Caterpillar, the caterpillar eats cake, pickles and sausage. What would you fix? | Real caterpillars eat the leaves of their own food plants | Nothing, caterpillars really can eat everything people eat | Caterpillars eat only fruit | Caterpillars eat only meat
test: The same book says the caterpillar builds a cocoon. What is the truth for a butterfly? | It makes a chrysalis; cocoons are spun by moths | It builds a nest of twigs | It digs a deep hole in the ground and sleeps there | It hides inside an egg again
test: A butterfly lands on your sleeve near the exit. What should you do? | Leave it be and ask a keeper to help it off | Grab it gently by its wings and lift it off yourself | Shake your arm hard | Blow on it until it flies
test: What would it be like to taste with your feet? | You would know if a leaf was right just by standing on it | You could only taste when barefoot | You would never need to eat with your mouth again | Your shoes would always taste of food
test: lol this moth is massive, how long does it live for? | Only a week or two, as it cannot eat | About ten years, just like a pet cat | About a year | Just one hour
test: Why does a glasswing hide so well? | Leaves and flowers show through its wings | It changes colour like a chameleon | It flies only at night | It hides inside flowers all day
test: Grandad says one brave monarch flies to Mexico and back every year. Right? | No, it takes several generations to fly north again | Yes, every single monarch does the whole round trip itself | No, monarchs never leave Canada | Yes, and it does it three times a year
test: How could a butterfly farm help protect a rainforest? | Local people earn money by keeping the forest standing | The butterflies plant new trees | The farm scares away the loggers with clouds of butterflies | Butterflies eat the weeds that kill trees
test: Your class wants to help red admirals at school. What could you leave in a corner? | A patch of nettles | A pile of plastic | A bowl of salt | A heap of sand
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the butterfly house is maintained at a high temperature. | Butterflies, being cold-blooded, require warmth in order to fly | Warmth deters the predatory birds which would otherwise eat them | Warmth prevents the scales from falling | Butterflies are incapable of perceiving cold
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the colouring of the blue morpho? | The blue derives from microscopic structures that reflect blue light | The blue derives from a pigment obtained from the flowers upon which it feeds | The blue is visible only by moonlight | The blue covers both surfaces of the wings
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the adult atlas moth. | It possesses no mouth and survives upon reserves laid down as a caterpillar | It consumes nectar in great quantity | It lives for a period of several years | It feeds exclusively upon rotting fruit
exam: It is given that five butterflies alight upon a dish. Ascertain the aggregate number of wings. | Twenty | Ten | Fifteen | Thirty
